The test cases in this directory are derived from the jsonata-js test suite:
https://github.com/jsonata-js/jsonata

THIS SUITE IS NO LONGER EXPECTED TO AGREE WITH jsonata-js EVERYWHERE.

jsntrs implements JSONata as *specified* — the JSONata language documentation
at jsonata.org, XPath 3.1 F&O for the picture-string builtins, and ECMAScript
for number-to-string. jsonata-js is evidence of what a behavior is meant to
be, never the authority: it has real bugs, and reproducing one is a defect
rather than conformance. Where an inherited case pins behavior the
specification contradicts, the expectation is re-derived from the
specification and the case is changed.

A case changed that way carries a `divergence` note saying why, with the
citation it rests on:

    "divergence": "jsonata-js indexes the active character in the whole
                   picture; XPath 3.1 F&O 4.7.4 indexes it within the
                   sub-picture"

Grep for it to find every deliberate departure from upstream. Groups named
`rust-*` were written for this project and were never part of the upstream
suite.

Two more note fields record where an expectation comes from. Neither is read
by the harness; both exist so that a later reader can tell a derived answer
from a recorded one without redoing the derivation:

    "authority":  the ranked source the expectation was derived from — a
                  quoted F&O or jsonata.org rule, or a W3C QT3 case id (see
                  testdata/oracles/qt3/). Check the citation, not the answer.

    "inherited":  the answer is jsonata-js's, adopted deliberately where the
                  ranked sources do not reach — a signature the reference
                  chose, a JSON.stringify argument it passes, a guard in its
                  replacer. The note names the mechanism. This is NOT the same
                  as "authority": nothing above jsonata-js was consulted, so
                  the case is evidence of parity, never of correctness.

    "unresolved": no authority fixes this answer. The case is a regression pin
                  over jsntrs' current behavior and must never be cited as
                  evidence that the behavior is correct. Changing it needs a
                  decision, not a bug report.

The distinction between the first two matters and was got wrong once already:
the wave-8 audit labelled three families `authority` while their notes plainly
said the provenance was the reference's implementation. The free text was
honest; the field name was not, and the field name is what a reader filters on.

A `rust-*` group whose cases carry `unresolved` notes also carries a README.md
stating the open question once, so the per-case notes can stay short.

